g  powers of parliament so  i think at the end of the day everybody  was very happy with the result  and yet some would say that 55 doesn't  go far enough  independent scientific analysts climate  action tracker for example says the eu  would need to set a target of  65 percent not 55 percent and fund  climate action abroad in order to have  commitments that are compatible with the  paris agreement  apart from the uk there's nobody else  on the planet that is doing as much as  the eu is doing  so in that sense i believe we're we're  together with the uk  we're setting a trend and now others are  following like united states and  hopefully also  other big emitters such as china will  follow a suit  and i believe if you put all these  efforts together  we could we could really get to climate  neutrality by the middle of the century  and i think  i think um we need to start immediately  and uh what we've done with the climate  law  compels the european union to stick to  that trajectory and i i believe  uh this is is is what needs to be done  also by other major emitters  at the end of the day frankly i don't  care who wins i mean everybody wins and  if the uk is ahead of the eu  good for them but we'll see each other  in 2050 and hopefully  both we will reach climate neutrality by  then  and you can see our full interview with  friends timmermans youtube.com  sky news let's take a look now at some  of the day's other climate news  and day two of the u.s president's  climate summit has been focusing on how  technology can help the world achieve a  net zero  climate-resilient economy joe biden  wants to spend  trillions of dollars on clean energy  technology to boost the us economy  and help create jobs while saving the  planet speakers at the summit today have  included bill gates michael bloomberg  and business executives  extinction rebellion activists have  dumped fake coal  outside the lloyd's headquarters in  london campaigners say  it's part of ongoing protests aimed at  exposing the insurance industry and its  investment in fossil fuel companies  lloyds of london says it plans to phase  out all existing insurance policies for  fossil fuel projects  by 2030.  [Applause]  scientists say crops could be naturally  modified to make them more resistant to  climate change  researchers found grass crops like wheat  can borrow genes from other species  the process known as lateral gene  transfer enables them to speed up  evolution  and adapt to new environments more  quickly  [Applause]  it looks set to be one of the driest  aprils on record the met office says  there's been an average of 12.8  millimeters of rain across the uk so far  this month  that's just 18 of average levels  it comes after the uk recorded the  coldest start to april  since 2013.  [Applause]  the habitats of endangered chimpanzees  will be studied by researchers at the  university of  leicester laser scanners are going to be  used to create  three-dimensional images of the primates  nests in the congo  the project will look at the forest and  woodlands in the nature reserve where  they live  climate change is driving migration  around the world  and in china's agricultural rural  communities  droughts in the northwest of the country  have become more frequent  they used to happen every four years but  since 1991  they now happen in this region every two  years in ningxia over the past  60 years temperatures have risen by 2.1  degrees  celsius with more than half of that  increase taking place  from 2001 to 2010. as a result more than  a million farmers have migrated  from the south to the north to the much  better  irrigated basin of the yellow river our  asia correspondent tom cheshire reports  now  from northwest china  [Applause]  this was once a thriving village  shihaiku in ningxia  north west china the un has described  this as one of the most  uninhabitable places in the world people  here still leaked out in existence for  years  carving homes out of the hills but  temperatures have risen two degrees over  the last half century  that's twice as fast as the global  average and a similar rise to what the  world is trying to avoid  with the paris climate agreement the  land simply could no longer  support the people who lived on it so  the village was abandoned  its houses destroyed this is a ghost  village and well it might look like a  relic of the past  it could be a glimpse of the future as  climate migration takes hold  worldwide this was always a tough place  to live but climate change  made it impossible the government  removed the last of the villagers in  2020  they went to this new town several  hundred miles north  they still need to work hard in the  fields but irrigation that was  impossible down south makes agriculture  here  much more reliable  mrs ma used to live in one of those cave  houses in shihaiku  and was one of the first to come here  around 20 years ago  [Music]  china is the world's biggest polluter  and it's already having to deal with the  effects of climate change  the same may be in store for the rest of  the world if it fails to meet its  targets  tom cheshire sky news ningxia teaching  children about climate change  isn't part of the national curriculum in  any country except italy  but a new international effort has been  launched to educate  three to seven-year-olds on sustainable  habits  it's called the earth warriors scheme  and dominic bond and his son billy are  taking part  are you an earth warrior yeah i am what  do you do when you're being an earth  warrior  i saved earth how do you help to save  the earth  by not catching by not using fish  by not using fish i mean not eating fish  okay well schweiterbari co-founded earth  warriors and she thinks education  is a powerful tool for combating climate  change  giving children the opportunity and the  knowledge that they need  to know about climate change the things  that they can do to combat it in the  future can  really empower them to be the future  change makers  it's not about teaching them anything  complex or scary  and it's very much framed in a positive  way around  building their love and bond with nature  now i just want to show you the  real-time power mix for great britain  because over the last couple of days  we've seen some of the highest solar  generation  levels of the year and the forecast for  this weekend is for near record levels  of solar power  wind generation remains low so overall  renewable output is around  one-third of the generation mix you can  see 38  there now solar power tends to be the  highest in spring  rather than summer when you might expect  it and that's because solar panels  perform better  in cooler temperatures now tomorrow  youth climate groups from around the  world will come together to launch  climate live  there'll be 24 hours of live music  streams with artists  activists and scientists all taking part  it's hoped the performances will help  unite people and reach those who aren't  currently engaged in the crisis  well earlier i spoke to singer declan  mckenna who's performing a special live  set for the event  for a long time now i've been feeling  the climate crisis is  has been vital for me to speak up about  and for me to be a part of campaigning  for  um and right now uh  really the important thing for me um is  holding the government  our government particularly supercam um  to  keeping you know global temperatures uh  below a 1.5 degree  increase i think that is a massively  important goal we're seeing the impacts  of  um you know pollution and rising  temperatures worldwide and  people are already suffering and what  about in terms of changing attitudes do  you feel that they  are changing i mean or do you think it's  a generational thing  what's your opinion it is one thing for  us individuals to make changes to our  lifestyle  and demand things it is another for our  governments  and our you know the systems that play  to to change  and to um become sustainable for  our future on this planet um so yes the  attitudes i i think even seeing the last  five years  um i've been very much engaged and you  know the change has been very obvious  obvious in  in our attitudes it's it's whether it  can  materialize with you know within within  the government  this year marks the 51st earth day the  world's largest environmental campaign  so let's take a look at how people  around the world have been celebrating  [Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  oh  [Music]  [Music]  well that's it for today's show but you  can get your climate fix over the  weekend with our podcast  on this week's episode more on those  earth day celebrations around the world  and what we learned from the leaders  climate summit hosted by president biden  climate cast is available wherever you  normally get your podcast from and of  course you can watch our weekly digital  show with becky cottrell on sky news  social channels our app  and our website thanks for watching and  we'll see you next week  you

